<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The DEFAULT locale's strings. English, because `lib/l10n/app_en.arb` is the
    template every other locale falls back to.

    ## Why this file exists at all, when the app is already localized

    Flutter's own localisation (`lib/l10n/app_*.arb`) only reaches text the Dart
    code renders. The **launcher label** is not one of those: it is read by the
    launcher process, from this APK's resources, through
    `PackageManager.getApplicationLabel()`, which resolves `android:label`
    against the **system** locale, before any Dart runs and regardless of the
    language chosen inside the app.

    So a build with `android:label="M1 Controller"` puts English under the icon
    on a Chinese phone, permanently, and no amount of ARB work changes it. That
    was `analysis/79` finding #16: the ARB already shipped `M1 控制器` while the
    one string a user sees while the app is closed stayed English.

    ## Only the label lives here

    Everything else in the UI is Flutter's business and is generated from the
    ARB files. Duplicating any other string here would create two sources of
    truth for it, and the copy in this file is the one that would silently go
    stale; nothing in `verify` compares these files with the ARB.

    The value below is therefore not invented: it is exactly `appTitle` from
    `lib/l10n/app_en.arb`, and `values-zh/strings.xml` is exactly `appTitle` from
    `lib/l10n/app_zh.arb`. `test/l10n_arb_test.dart` owns ARB parity; the
    Android side is checked by `tools/audit_app_capabilities.py`, which reads the
    merged manifest and fails if `android:label` stops being a string resource.

    ## XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment

    These comments use `;` and `:` where a dash would be natural. That is not a
    style choice: XML's comment production excludes that two-character sequence.
    Gradle's resource merger enforces it, and so does `aapt2 compile` once the
    file is a `values/` resource rather than a bare document. The first version
    of this file used it twice, once as punctuation and once inside the name of
    an aapt2 command-line flag, and failed the release build at
    `:app:packageReleaseResources` with a SAXParseException. This paragraph
    therefore spells the sequence out in words rather than showing it.
-->
<resources>
    <string name="app_name">M1 Controller</string>
</resources>
